# Installer appliance profile: a bootable USB environment whose
# sole purpose is creating Saphira Linux installations onto a separate
# real disk via the console creator menu.
#
# Intentions ONLY. The package manager owns dependency closure; never
# enumerate resolved dependencies here (saphira-profiles(5)).
#
# This profile describes the appliance, never its products: the
# product, firmware, filesystem, and hostname are chosen in the menu
# at runtime and written into the INSTALLED system only. Credentials,
# users, networking, and DNS are never chosen here: destinations boot
# locked-root into ordinary saphira-firstboot. Each entry earns its place:
#
#   saphira-base        libc/loader, filesystem layout, apk, shell, core
#                       runtime, account and package tooling.
#   saphira-kernel      the kernel and its modules. Boot is impossible
#                       without it. The appliance itself boots ext4.
#   systemd             the init system for this appliance; the creator
#                       menu ships as a systemd unit and owns tty1.
#                       The installed system chooses its own init independently.
#   grub                both bootloader targets (i386-pc and EFI
#                       removable) are installed into the appliance
#                       image at creation.
#   e2fsprogs           mkfs.ext4/fsck for ext4 roots.
#   dosfstools          mkfs.vfat for the ESP.
#   dialog              the menu system. No menu without it.
#   udev                the device manager: /dev/disk/by-id never
#                       exists without it (proven Egg 2026-10-19), and
#                       the menu refuses to run without persistent
#                       disk identifiers.
#   bash                the appliance shell scripts run under bash.
#   vim                 console editor for install manifests.
#   iproute2            ip for the menu network status and static
#                       address setup (saphira-network is absent here).
#   findutils           file discovery used by installer tooling
#                       (saphira-network is absent here).
#   iputils             ping: the menu refuses a write unless the
#                       repository host answers (3-second reply timeout
#                       per family).
#   util-linux          sfdisk, wipefs, blockdev, lsblk, blkid:
#                       partitioning, safety checks and real-disk writes.
#   kmod                modprobe/insmod: the appliance loads the ZFS
#                       module itself before creating pools.
#   saphira-image-creator
#                       the image build engine the menu drives.
#   saphira-install-tools
#                       profiles, planner, installer and menu tooling.
#   saphira-zfs-7.2.3   ZFS kmod matching the appliance kernel: the
#                       appliance must create pools, so the module lives
#                       here, not only in its products.
#   saphira-zfs-userspace
#                       zpool/zfs binaries for pool creation.
#
# Deliberately absent, each a decision rather than an oversight:
#   - saphira-image-firstboot: no wizard runs here; the menu owns the
#     console and destinations run ordinary firstboot themselves;
#   - saphira-network and dhcpcd: systemd-networkd owns the appliance
#     NIC through the creator's wired DHCP default; destinations carry
#     their own network stack and firstboot;
#   - saphira-firstboot on the appliance: the binary may ship with the
#     base, but its marker is never armed here;
#   - openssh and passwords: the appliance has an empty root, no SSH,
#     and no password prompts; destination credentials belong to the
#     destination's firstboot, never to this menu;
#   - a graphical stack of any kind (see the headless policy).
saphira-base
saphira-kernel-7.2.3
systemd
grub
e2fsprogs
dosfstools
dialog
udev
bash
vim
iproute2
findutils
iputils
util-linux
kmod
saphira-image-creator
saphira-install-tools
saphira-zfs-7.2.3
saphira-zfs-userspace
